2. Never treat `/tmp`, browser storage, or a process-local Map as authoritative production persistence.
3. Fail closed if the server cannot verify an elevated session. Do not treat a network error as proof of authorization.
4. Apply expiration, attempt limits, resend cooldowns, replay prevention, rate limiting, and atomic verification to authentication challenges.
5. Verify the Firebase ID token and the elevated admin session where required. Neither one should accidentally substitute for the other.
6. Validate request payloads with appropriate schemas and reject invalid, oversized, or unexpected inputs.
7. Apply consistent error handling and appropriate HTTP status codes to every API endpoint.
8. Ensure a failed email send or failed database update never returns a false success response.
9. Do not log passwords, OTPs, API keys, service-account private keys, full session tokens, or other credentials.
10. Verify the exact Firebase project and Firestore database ID from the actual application configuration. Remove hardcoded project/database assumptions when configuration should be authoritative.
11. Preserve useful request IDs and sanitized operational logs.
12. Review rate limits and security controls for login, OTP, admin setup, file uploads, contact submissions, and registration endpoints.

Never print secret values into logs, test reports, GitHub commits, or client responses. If credentials have previously been exposed, document the need to rotate them through their provider dashboards.

